Ecological Inheritance in Artificial Societies: An Information-Theoretic Framework for Stigmergic Technological Evolution

Abstract

Recent empirical work with large populations of language-model agents has demonstrated that collective technological organization can emerge without predefined roles or direct agent-to-agent communication. The SwarmWorld platform demonstrates that agents interacting solely through a persistent, materially constrained environment can produce executable technologies that evolve through multi-author lineages and remain functional after all agents are removed. The Praxist system has further demonstrated that cumulative artifact lineages can achieve benchmark-dominant performance in autonomous research and development tasks through structured environmental inheritance mechanisms. These observations motivate a formal theoretical account of what we term ecological inheritance in artificial societies.

We build on niche construction theory from evolutionary biology, adapting its core concept of ecological inheritance to the domain of multi-agent artificial intelligence. We define a general framework in which a persistent environment serves not merely as a communication medium or external memory, but as a substrate for the transmission, preservation, modification, and cumulative evolution of functional information across generations of agents.

Our central contribution is a rigorous information-theoretic characterization of this process. We provide a computable approximation to functional information suitable for technological artifacts, grounded in Kolmogorov complexity and compression-based estimation. We state a formal theorem specifying sufficient conditions—persistence, observability, functional relevance, and modification capacity—under which functional information propagates through environmental artifacts without direct agent-to-agent communication. We derive explicit convergence bounds on the rate of performance improvement in artifact lineages and establish information-theoretic limits on inheritance capacity.

We further distinguish three levels of collective information propagation—direct communication, stigmergic coordination, and cumulative technological inheritance—and demonstrate that the transition from Level II to Level III requires specific architectural conditions that are not guaranteed by stigmergy alone. We address the philosophical status of environmentally distributed cognition, arguing that artificial societies possess a unique "mark of the cognitive" grounded in evaluative constitution rather than representational origin. Finally, we formulate a bounded environmental amplification conjecture with explicit failure modes and boundary conditions, providing a falsifiable framework for testing the limits of collective intelligence in artificial systems.

The framework clarifies that collective intelligence is not confined to the computational processes of individual agents, but is distributed across agents, environmental states, technological artifacts, and the lineages connecting those artifacts. We position this work as providing the theoretical foundations that empirical systems such as Praxist and SwarmWorld instantiate, and derive from it concrete design principles for constructing artificial societies capable of cumulative technological evolution.

---

1. Introduction

The capacity of collectives to solve problems without central control or direct communication has long been studied through the concept of stigmergy. In stigmergic systems, agents coordinate indirectly by modifying a shared environment; subsequent agents perceive those modifications and adjust their behavior accordingly. This mechanism is well documented in social insects and has been repeatedly implemented in artificial multi-agent reinforcement learning.

Recent work with language-model agents has revealed a qualitatively stronger phenomenon. SwarmWorld presents a multi-agent system in which initially homogeneous LLM agents operate in a persistent, physically constrained environment. Under a condition of "No Explicit Culture"—that is, with no direct communication or inherited instruction sets—agents nonetheless construct durable artifacts and write executable controllers. These technologies evolve through multi-author forks and instruction-level edits, forming lineages that persist beyond the lifetimes of their creators. Agents are then removed, and the technological state is subjected to held-out disturbances to measure post-agent functional persistence; the system exhibits non-trivial resilience.

Even more strikingly, the Praxist system demonstrates that cumulative artifact lineages in autonomous research and development can achieve performance exceeding human expert baselines on complex tasks including machine learning engineering benchmarks, rocket landing control, and quantitative finance. Praxist formalizes a generational state transition system with typed evidence graphs, durable lessons compressed into reusable "Gems," and a full inheritance cycle from artifact discovery through frontier expansion to lineage compression. This represents the most sophisticated engineering instantiation to date of environmental inheritance in artificial societies.

These empirical demonstrations motivate a pressing theoretical question: what are the necessary and sufficient conditions under which a population of artificial agents can generate an evolving technological substrate that preserves, propagates, and amplifies functional intelligence beyond the lifetime of its individual creators? The present paper develops a formal theoretical framework for this regime, which we term ecological inheritance in artificial societies, deliberately invoking the established biological theory of niche construction.

Our contribution is threefold. First, we provide an information-theoretic characterization of environmental inheritance in artificial societies, specifying precise conditions under which functional information propagates through persistent environmental artifacts without direct agent-to-agent communication. Second, we derive explicit bounds on the rate of performance improvement in artifact lineages and establish information-theoretic limits on inheritance capacity. Third, we formulate a bounded environmental amplification conjecture with explicit failure modes, providing a falsifiable framework for testing the limits of collective intelligence.

The resulting theoretical question is no longer simply whether AI agents can communicate or cooperate. It becomes: under what mathematical conditions can a population of artificial agents generate an evolving technological substrate that preserves, propagates, and amplifies functional intelligence beyond the lifetime of its individual creators?

