3. Formal Framework

Let a multi-agent society be represented by the tuple:

\mathcal{S} = (A, E, \mathcal{T}, G, \Pi, \mathcal{M})

where:

· $A = \{a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_N\}$ is a finite or countable set of agents;
· $E$ is the persistent environment state space, which includes both physical configurations and non-technological informational traces;
· $\mathcal{T}$ is the set of technological artifacts (a subset of the environment that has executable or functional structure);
· $G$ describes the interaction graph and environmental coupling, including observation and action channels;
· $\Pi = \{\pi_1, \ldots, \pi_N\}$ is the set of individual behavioral policies;
· $\mathcal{M}: \mathcal{T} \times A \rightarrow \Delta(\mathcal{T})$ is a stochastic artifact modification operator, specifying how an agent transforms an existing artifact into a new one.

Each agent $a_i \in A$ observes a local state:

o_i(t) = O_i(E_t, T_t)

and selects an action:

u_i(t) = \pi_i(o_i(t))

which may include creating, modifying, or using an artifact. The environment evolves according to:

E_{t+1} = F(E_t, u_1(t), \ldots, u_N(t))

No direct communication between agents is required:

C_{ij} = 0, \quad i \neq j

where $C_{ij}$ denotes any direct information channel. Nevertheless, an action performed by one agent can modify the environment in a persistent and observable manner. A subsequent agent can acquire information indirectly through the state of the world:

a_i \rightarrow E \rightarrow a_j

This is environment-mediated information transmission.

Technological artifacts $T_t \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ are part of the environment but possess internal dynamics—they can be executed, produce outputs, and be recombined. We assume a countable space of possible artifacts $\mathcal{T}$ with a complexity measure $c(T)$, such as description length in a fixed grammar or Kolmogorov complexity relative to a reference machine.

We further define a generational structure on artifact lineages. A lineage is a sequence:

T_0 \rightarrow T_1 \rightarrow T_2 \rightarrow \cdots \rightarrow T_n

where each transition $T_k \rightarrow T_{k+1}$ is mediated by at least one agent applying the modification operator $\mathcal{M}$. The lineage may involve multiple agents, and no single agent need persist across generations.

The notion of a "generation" requires clarification in this context. In biological evolution, generations are defined by reproduction. In artificial societies, we define a generation as a discrete step in the artifact lineage where a new artifact is created or an existing artifact is significantly modified. This is analogous to the distinction between parent and offspring in genetic inheritance, but applied to technological artifacts rather than organisms.

