6. Three Levels of Collective Information Propagation

We refine the original three-level classification with formal information-theoretic criteria.

Level I: Direct Communication.

Information is transmitted directly between agents:

I(A_i ; A_j \mid E, T) > 0

This corresponds to traditional communication-based coordination, where agents exchange information through explicit messages.

Level II: Stigmergic Coordination.

Information is encoded in environmental modifications and retrieved by later agents:

I(A_i ; E_{t+1} \mid A_j) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad I(E_{t+1} ; A_j) > 0

but there is no direct artifact-to-artifact transmission independent of agents:

I(T_k ; T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k) = 0

This corresponds to coordination without cumulative inheritance—agents use the environment as a communication medium, but artifacts do not evolve through lineages.

Level III: Cumulative Technological Inheritance.

Information flows from artifact to artifact across generations, even when conditioning on the agent population and environment:

I(T_k ; T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k) > 0

This is the regime of our theorem. Level III subsumes Level II but adds the requirement that the environment itself carries a heritable signal that persists and evolves independently of any single agent's intervention.

The formal distinction between Level II and Level III requires careful interpretation. In both cases, agents are the causal intermediaries between successive artifacts. The distinction is whether the artifact itself carries information that influences its own evolution, beyond what the agents would produce de novo. In Level II, the artifact is merely a communication channel between agents; in Level III, the artifact becomes a heritable substrate that accumulates information over time.

We note that the transition from Level II to Level III is not automatic. A system can exhibit stigmergic coordination without cumulative inheritance if artifacts are not persistent, observable, functionally relevant, or modifiable. The four conditions of our theorem specify when the transition to Level III occurs.

