7. Bounded Environmental Amplification Conjecture

We now state a refined, falsifiable version of the amplification conjecture, incorporating boundary conditions and failure modes.

Let $I_{\text{collective}}(t)$ be a measurable operationalization of collective intelligence, such as the total functional information of the artifact pool or task-solving performance on a benchmark. Let $\max_i I_i(t)$ be the maximum individual agent intelligence. We conjecture that there exists a regime in which:

\frac{\partial I_{\text{collective}}}{\partial M_E} > 0

and:

\lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} \frac{I_{\text{collective}}(t)}{\max_i I_i(t)} \rightarrow \infty

provided that the following boundary conditions hold:

Condition 1 (Non-saturation). The environment does not saturate; that is, $M_E$ grows without bound or is efficiently compressed. If environmental memory is finite and non-compressible, the ratio plateaus.

Condition 2 (Sufficient modification capacity). The modification capacity $K$ remains above the complexity of new functional challenges. If $K$ is insufficient, artifacts cannot be improved, and the process stagnates.

Condition 3 (Favorable non-stationarity). The process is non-stationary in a favorable direction—no catastrophic forgetting or destructive interference. If the environment changes in ways that destroy accumulated artifacts, the process regresses.

We further identify three failure modes:

Failure Mode 1 (Saturation). When $M_E$ is bounded, $I_{\text{collective}}$ plateaus. This follows from the fact that finite memory can only store finite information, and the information content of the artifact pool is bounded by the environmental capacity.

Failure Mode 2 (Destructive interference). When modification capacity $K$ is too high relative to selection pressure, artifacts become chaotic. This connects to non-stationarity results from MEDAL-ADR, which showed that cultural transmission collapses without careful curriculum design to handle distribution shifts.

Failure Mode 3 (Catastrophic forgetting). When agent policies $\pi_i$ are updated online, inherited information is overwritten. This is the analog of catastrophic forgetting in continual learning, applied to cultural evolution in artificial societies.

This conjecture is testable by varying $M_E$ and $K$ in simulation and measuring the ratio. It predicts that under ideal conditions, the collective can exceed the ceiling of any single agent, echoing the concept of the extended mind but with an evolutionary dimension.

