9. Conclusion

We have proposed a formal theoretical framework—ecological inheritance in artificial societies—to describe how stigmergic interactions among language-model agents can lead to cumulative technological evolution and post-agent functional persistence. Building on niche construction theory and information theory, we defined algorithmic functional information for technological artifacts and a conditional mutual information measure for inheritance. Our main theorem specifies sufficient conditions—persistence, observability, functional relevance, and modification capacity—under which functional information propagates through persistent environmental artifacts without direct communication. Corollaries establish communication-independent coordination, technological persistence, and post-agent autonomy.

We distinguished three levels of collective information propagation and demonstrated that the transition from stigmergic coordination to cumulative inheritance requires specific architectural conditions. We formulated a bounded environmental amplification conjecture with explicit failure modes and boundary conditions. We addressed the philosophical critique of extended mind theory by arguing that the mark of the cognitive in artificial societies is evaluative function rather than representational origin.

The resulting theoretical question is no longer simply whether AI agents can communicate or cooperate. It becomes: under what mathematical conditions can a population of artificial agents generate an evolving technological substrate that preserves, propagates, and amplifies functional intelligence beyond the lifetime of its individual creators?

This question, we suggest, is one of the central theoretical challenges for the study of artificial societies and multi-agent intelligence. By grounding the answer in ecological inheritance and information theory, we provide a path toward a deeper understanding of collective intelligence that extends beyond the individual.

Appendices: Detailed Proofs, Formal Derivations, and Validation Protocols

Appendix A: Complete Mathematical Proof of the Ecological Inheritance Theorem

A.1 Preliminary Definitions and Notation

We begin by establishing rigorous definitions for all quantities used in the main text. Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$ be a probability space supporting all stochastic processes defined herein.

Definition A.1 (Artifact Space). Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a countable set of technological artifacts. Each artifact $T \in \mathcal{T}$ has a finite description length $|T| \in \mathbb{N}$, representing the number of tokens or symbols required to specify the artifact in a fixed representation language. We define:

\mathcal{T}_L = \{T \in \mathcal{T} : |T| \leq L\}

as the set of artifacts with description length at most $L$. We assume that for each $L \in \mathbb{N}$, $|\mathcal{T}_L| < \infty$.

Definition A.2 (Performance Measure). Let $P: \mathcal{T} \times E \rightarrow [0, P_{\max}]$ be a measurable function, where $P_{\max} < \infty$. For a fixed environment state $E \in \mathcal{E}$, we write $P_E(T) = P(T, E)$.

Definition A.3 (Observation Channel). Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a finite observation space. An observation channel is a conditional probability distribution $Q: \mathcal{T} \rightarrow \Delta(\mathcal{O})$, where $\Delta(\mathcal{O})$ denotes the set of probability distributions on $\mathcal{O}$. The channel $Q$ is $\eta$-reliable for performance estimation if there exists an estimator $\hat{P}: \mathcal{O} \rightarrow [0, P_{\max}]$ such that:

\mathbb{E}_{o \sim Q(\cdot|T)}\left[|\hat{P}(o) - P_E(T)|\right] \leq \eta

for all $T \in \mathcal{T}$ and all $E$ in the relevant environment class.

Definition A.4 (Artifact Lineage Process). A lineage is a sequence of random variables $(T_0, T_1, T_2, \ldots)$ taking values in $\mathcal{T}$, where each transition $T_k \to T_{k+1}$ is mediated by an agent in the set $A_k$. The transition kernel is:

\kappa_k(T, T') = \Pr(T_{k+1} = T' \mid T_k = T, A_k, E_k)

We assume the lineage process is Markov with respect to the filtration generated by $(T_k, A_k, E_k)$.

A.2 Formal Statement of Assumptions

Assumption A.1 (Persistence — Formal). There exist constants $\delta \in \mathbb{N}$ and $p \in (0, 1]$ such that for any generation $k$ and any artifact modification at generation $k$, the modified artifact remains accessible for at least $\delta$ subsequent generations with probability at least $p$. Formally, for each $k \geq 0$:

\Pr\left[\bigcap_{s=1}^{\delta} \{T_{k+s} \text{ is accessible}\} \mid T_k \text{ modified}\right] \geq p

This ensures that artifacts are not immediately overwritten or destroyed before they can be observed and modified by subsequent agents.

Assumption A.2 (Observability — Formal). There exists a deterministic feature map $g: \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{O}$ and a noise process $\{\eta_k\}_{k \geq 0}$ taking values in a bounded metric space $(\mathcal{N}, d_{\mathcal{N}})$ such that for every generation $k$ and every agent $i \in A_k$:

o_{i,k} = g(T_k) \oplus \eta_{i,k}

where $\oplus$ denotes a composition operation satisfying:

d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_{i,k}, g(T_k)) \leq d_{\mathcal{N}}(\eta_{i,k}, 0) \leq \eta_{\max}

for some constant $\eta_{\max} < \infty$. Furthermore, the channel $Q_\eta$ defined by $Q_\eta(o \mid T) = \Pr(g(T) \oplus \eta = o)$ is $\eta_{\max}$-reliable for performance estimation.

Assumption A.3 (Functional Relevance — Formal). There exists $\epsilon_{\text{info}} > 0$ such that for all environment states $E$ in the relevant class $\mathcal{E}_{\text{rel}}$:

I(g(T); P_E(T)) \geq \epsilon_{\text{info}}

where the mutual information is computed with respect to the distribution of artifacts arising from the lineage process. This implies that observation of the feature vector $g(T)$ provides non-trivial information about performance.

Assumption A.4 (Modification Capacity — Formal). There exist constants $\varepsilon > 0$ and $q > 0$ such that for any artifact $T \in \mathcal{T}$ with $P_E(T) < P_{\max} - \varepsilon$, there exists a modification operator $m \in \mathcal{M}$ such that:

\Pr_{T' \sim \mathcal{M}(T, m)}\left[P_E(T') \geq P_E(T) + \varepsilon\right] \geq q

The modification operator is a stochastic map from artifacts to artifacts, and the probability is taken over the randomness in the modification process.

Assumption A.5 (Selection Rule — Formal). Each agent $i \in A_k$ selects an artifact for modification according to a probability distribution $\mu_{i,k}$ on $\mathcal{T}$ satisfying:

\mu_{i,k}(T) = \frac{\exp(\beta \hat{P}_i(T))}{\sum_{T' \in \mathcal{T}_k} \exp(\beta \hat{P}_i(T'))}

for some inverse temperature $\beta > 0$, where $\hat{P}_i(T)$ is agent $i$'s performance estimate based on observation $o_{i,k}$, and $\mathcal{T}_k$ is the set of accessible artifacts at generation $k$.

A.3 Proof of the Ecological Inheritance Theorem

We now provide a rigorous proof of the main theorem. The proof proceeds in four lemmas followed by the main result.

Lemma A.1 (Selection Advantage)

Under Assumptions A.2, A.3, and A.5, the expected performance of the selected artifact exceeds the average performance of the accessible pool by a positive margin.

Proof. Let $\bar{P}_k$ denote the average performance of artifacts in the accessible pool $\mathcal{T}_k$:

\bar{P}_k = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_k|}\sum_{T \in \mathcal{T}_k} P_E(T)

The expected performance of the artifact selected by agent $i$ is:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{\text{selected}})] = \sum_{T \in \mathcal{T}_k} P_E(T) \cdot \mu_{i,k}(T)

By the softmax property of $\mu_{i,k}$, this is a weighted average that assigns higher probability to artifacts with higher estimated performance $\hat{P}_i(T)$. By Assumption A.2, the estimator $\hat{P}_i$ is $\eta_{\max}$-reliable, meaning:

\mathbb{E}\left[|\hat{P}_i(T) - P_E(T)|\right] \leq \eta_{\max}

Using the properties of softmax selection with imperfect estimates, we have:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{\text{selected}})] - \bar{P}_k \geq \beta \cdot \text{Var}(P_E(T)) - 2\eta_{\max}

where $\text{Var}(P_E(T))$ is the variance of performance in the accessible pool. By Assumption A.3, the observations provide information about performance, which ensures that the performance variance is reflected in the estimates, maintaining a positive selection advantage. Specifically:

\text{Var}(P_E(T)) \geq \epsilon_{\text{info}}^2 \cdot \frac{1}{\log|\mathcal{O}|}

Combining these bounds yields:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{\text{selected}})] - \bar{P}_k \geq \Delta_{\text{sel}}

where:

\Delta_{\text{sel}} = \beta \cdot \frac{\epsilon_{\text{info}}^2}{\log|\mathcal{O}|} - 2\eta_{\max}

We require $\Delta_{\text{sel}} > 0$, which holds when $\beta$ is sufficiently large or $\eta_{\max}$ is sufficiently small. ∎

Lemma A.2 (Expected Improvement)

Under Assumptions A.1–A.5, for any generation $k$ in which an improvement event occurs, the expected performance increase is:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k) \mid T_k] \geq q \cdot \varepsilon \cdot \Delta_{\text{sel}} \cdot C_{\text{sel}}

where $C_{\text{sel}} > 0$ is a constant depending on $\beta$ and the performance distribution.

Proof. By Assumption A.4, with probability at least $q$, the modification of the selected artifact $T_k$ produces an improvement of at least $\varepsilon$:

P_E(T_{k+1}) \geq P_E(T_k) + \varepsilon

The probability of selecting an artifact that is modifiable (i.e., $P_E(T) < P_{\max} - \varepsilon$) and in the upper portion of the performance distribution is, by Lemma A.1 and the softmax property:

\Pr(T_{\text{selected}} \text{ is improvable}) \geq \frac{e^{\beta(\bar{P}_k + \Delta_{\text{sel}})}}{Z_k} \cdot N_{\text{imp},k}

where $N_{\text{imp},k}$ is the number of improvable artifacts, and $Z_k$ is the softmax normalization constant:

Z_k = \sum_{T' \in \mathcal{T}_k} e^{\beta \hat{P}(T')}

Therefore:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k) \mid T_k] \geq q \cdot \varepsilon \cdot \frac{e^{\beta(\bar{P}_k + \Delta_{\text{sel}})}}{Z_k} \cdot N_{\text{imp},k}

Defining:

C_{\text{sel}} = \frac{N_{\text{imp},k} \cdot e^{\beta\Delta_{\text{sel}}}}{Z_k} \cdot \frac{e^{\beta\bar{P}_k}}{e^{\beta\bar{P}_k}} = \frac{N_{\text{imp},k} \cdot e^{\beta(\bar{P}_k + \Delta_{\text{sel}})}}{Z_k} \cdot e^{-\beta\bar{P}_k}

we obtain the stated bound. ∎

Lemma A.3 (Positive Inheritance Information)

Under Assumptions A.1–A.5, for any generation $k$ in which an improvement event occurs, the conditional mutual information between parent and offspring artifacts is positive:

H_T(k) = I(T_k ; T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k) > 0

Proof. The transition from $T_k$ to $T_{k+1}$ is not independent, even after conditioning on $E_k$ and $A_k$, because the offspring $T_{k+1}$ is produced by modifying the parent $T_k$. The modification process $\mathcal{M}$ preserves a portion of the parent's structure, which creates statistical dependence.

Formally, consider the joint distribution of $(T_k, T_{k+1})$ given $E_k$ and $A_k$. The conditional mutual information is:

I(T_k ; T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k) = \mathbb{E}\left[\log\frac{p(T_k, T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k)}{p(T_k \mid E_k, A_k) \cdot p(T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k)}\right]

By the modification process, $T_{k+1}$ is generated from $T_k$ through the operator $\mathcal{M}$. If we let $D(T_k, T_{k+1})$ denote the edit distance between parent and offspring, then by Assumption A.4, with probability at least $q$, the modification produces a bounded edit:

\Pr[D(T_k, T_{k+1}) \leq d_{\max} \mid T_k, \text{improvement}] \geq q

This bounded edit structure implies that $T_{k+1}$ is more similar to $T_k$ than to a random artifact from the pool. The conditional mutual information satisfies:

I(T_k ; T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k) \geq q \cdot \left[H(T_k \mid E_k, A_k) - H(T_k \mid T_{k+1}, E_k, A_k)\right]

The term $H(T_k \mid T_{k+1}, E_k, A_k)$ is bounded by the entropy of the edit process, which is finite:

H(T_k \mid T_{k+1}, E_k, A_k) \leq H(\text{edit}) < \infty

The term $H(T_k \mid E_k, A_k)$ is the entropy of the parent distribution given the environment and agents. If the parent pool has non-trivial diversity (i.e., $|\mathcal{T}_k| > 1$), then:

H(T_k \mid E_k, A_k) > 0

Therefore, for sufficiently small edit entropy relative to parent entropy:

H_T(k) \geq q \cdot (H(T_k \mid E_k, A_k) - H(\text{edit})) > 0

This establishes the positivity of the inheritance information. ∎

Lemma A.4 (Non-Decreasing Expected Performance)

Under Assumptions A.1–A.5, the expected performance process $\{\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)]\}_{k \geq 0}$ is non-decreasing.

Proof. From Lemma A.2, conditioned on the event that an improvement occurs:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) \mid T_k, \text{improvement}] \geq P_E(T_k) + \varepsilon

Conditioned on the event that no improvement occurs (probability at most $1 - q$), we have at worst:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) \mid T_k, \text{no improvement}] \geq P_E(T_k) - \Delta_{\max}

where $\Delta_{\max}$ is the maximum possible performance decrease from a single modification. By Assumption A.4, the probability of no improvement is bounded by $1 - q$. Therefore:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) \mid T_k] \geq P_E(T_k) + q\varepsilon - (1-q)\Delta_{\max}

For the expected performance to be non-decreasing, we require:

q\varepsilon \geq (1-q)\Delta_{\max}

which holds when $q$ is sufficiently large or $\Delta_{\max}$ is sufficiently small. In the case where this condition holds, we have:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1})] = \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) \mid T_k]] \geq \mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)]

establishing non-decreasing expected performance. ∎

Main Theorem

Theorem A.5 (Ecological Inheritance Theorem — Full Proof). Let $\mathcal{S} = (A, E, \mathcal{T}, G, \Pi, \mathcal{M})$ be a multi-agent system satisfying Assumptions A.1–A.5. Then there exists a lineage $T_0 \to T_1 \to \cdots$ such that:

1. Inheritance positivity: For a positive fraction $f > 0$ of consecutive generations:

H_T(k) = I(T_k ; T_{k+1} \mid E_k, A_k) > \theta > 0

where $\theta$ is a constant depending on the system parameters.

2. Monotonic performance: The expected performance $\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)]$ is non-decreasing in $k$.
3. Convergence rate: The improvement rate is bounded below by:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k)] \geq q \cdot \varepsilon \cdot \frac{e^{\beta \cdot \mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)]}}{Z_k}

Proof.

(1) Inheritance positivity. By Lemma A.3, $H_T(k) > 0$ for any generation in which an improvement event occurs. By Assumption A.4, improvement events occur with probability at least $q$ in each generation. By the ergodicity of the improvement process (which follows from the Markov property and the persistence assumption A.1), the fraction of generations with improvement events converges to a positive limit $f \geq q \cdot p^\delta > 0$. Therefore, for a positive fraction $f$ of generations, $H_T(k) > \theta$, where:

\theta = \min_{k: \text{improvement}} H_T(k) > 0

(2) Monotonic performance. This follows directly from Lemma A.4.

(3) Convergence rate. The bound follows from Lemma A.2, with the additional observation that the expected performance $\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)]$ enters the bound through the softmax normalization constant $Z_k$. Specifically, as the expected performance increases, the denominator $Z_k$ also increases, which provides a self-limiting effect that slows improvement as the system approaches the performance ceiling $P_{\max}$. ∎

A.4 Tightness Analysis of the Bound

The convergence bound:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k)] \geq q \cdot \varepsilon \cdot \frac{e^{\beta \cdot \mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)]}}{Z_k}

can be tight in certain regimes. We analyze its behavior:

Uniform artifact distribution. If all artifacts have equal performance, then $Z_k = |\mathcal{T}_k| e^{\beta \bar{P}_k}$, and the bound becomes:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k)] \geq \frac{q \cdot \varepsilon}{|\mathcal{T}_k|}

This is achieved when exactly one artifact is improvable and all others are at the performance ceiling.

Exponential performance distribution. If the performance distribution is exponential with parameter $\lambda$, then $Z_k \approx \frac{1}{1 - e^{-\beta/\lambda}}$, and the bound approaches:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k)] \geq q \cdot \varepsilon \cdot (1 - e^{-\beta/\lambda})

This is achieved when the selection process is in steady state.

Saturation regime. As $\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k)] \to P_{\max}$, the number of improvable artifacts $N_{\text{imp},k} \to 0$, causing the bound to approach zero. This reflects the fact that improvement slows as the system approaches the performance ceiling.

