Appendix C: Convergence Analysis and Martingale Theory

C.1 Martingale Formulation of the Performance Process

We analyze the convergence of the performance process using martingale theory.

Definition C.1 (Performance Martingale). Define the process:

X_k = P_E(T_k) - \mathbb{E}[P_E(T_k) \mid \mathcal{F}_{k-1}]

where $\mathcal{F}_k$ is the filtration generated by $(T_0, \ldots, T_k, A_0, \ldots, A_k, E_0, \ldots, E_k)$. The process $X_k$ is a martingale difference sequence.

Lemma C.2 (Bounded Increments). Under Assumptions A.1–A.5, the martingale differences are bounded:

|X_k| \leq \Delta_{\max} < \infty

Proof. The performance of any artifact is bounded in $[0, P_{\max}]$. Therefore, $|P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k)| \leq P_{\max}$, and the same bound applies to $|X_{k+1}|$. ∎

Theorem C.3 (Almost Sure Convergence). The process $P_E(T_k)$ converges almost surely to a limiting random variable $P_\infty$.

Proof. By Lemma C.2, the martingale differences are bounded. By the Azuma-Hoeffding inequality, for any $\epsilon > 0$:

\Pr\left[\left|\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i\right| > \epsilon\right] \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{\epsilon^2}{2n\Delta_{\max}^2}\right)

By the Borel-Cantelli lemma, the partial sums $\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i$ converge almost surely. Since:

P_E(T_k) = \mathbb{E}[P_E(T_0)] + \sum_{i=1}^{k} X_i + \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathbb{E}[P_E(T_i) - P_E(T_{i-1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}]

and by Lemma A.4, the drift term $\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_i) - P_E(T_{i-1}) \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}]$ is non-negative and bounded, the process converges almost surely. ∎

C.2 Convergence Rate via Drift Analysis

We derive explicit convergence rates using drift analysis.

Theorem C.4 (Convergence Rate). Suppose that in addition to Assumptions A.1–A.5, the improvement bound satisfies:

\mathbb{E}[P_E(T_{k+1}) - P_E(T_k) \mid T_k] \geq \alpha \cdot (P_{\max} - P_E(T_k))

for some $\alpha > 0$. Then the expected performance converges linearly to $P_{\max}$:

\mathbb{E}[P_{\max} - P_E(T_k)] \leq (1 - \alpha)^k \cdot \mathbb{E}[P_{\max} - P_E(T_0)]

Proof. Define the gap process $G_k = P_{\max} - P_E(T_k)$. Under the assumption:

\mathbb{E}[G_{k+1} \mid G_k] \leq G_k - \alpha G_k = (1 - \alpha) G_k

Taking expectations and iterating:

\mathbb{E}[G_k] \leq (1 - \alpha)^k \mathbb{E}[G_0]

This establishes linear convergence. ∎

Corollary C.5. Under the conditions of Theorem C.4, the number of generations required to achieve performance within $\epsilon$ of $P_{\max}$ is:

K(\epsilon) = \frac{\log(\mathbb{E}[G_0] / \epsilon)}{\log(1/(1-\alpha))} \approx \frac{1}{\alpha}\log\frac{\mathbb{E}[G_0]}{\epsilon}

for small $\alpha$.

C.3 Concentration Inequalities for Inheritance Fraction

We establish concentration bounds on the fraction $f$ of generations with positive inheritance.

Theorem C.6 (Concentration of Inheritance Fraction). Let $f_n$ denote the empirical fraction of generations $k \in \{0, \ldots, n-1\}$ with $H_T(k) > \theta$. Then for any $\epsilon > 0$:

\Pr\left[|f_n - f| > \epsilon\right] \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{n\epsilon^2}{2}\right)

where $f = \mathbb{E}[f_n] \geq q \cdot p^\delta$ is the expected fraction.

Proof. By Assumption A.4 and A.1, the probability of an improvement event in any generation is at least $q \cdot p^\delta$. By Lemma A.3, every improvement event results in $H_T(k) > \theta$. The indicator variables $\mathbf{1}\{H_T(k) > \theta\}$ are not independent (due to the Markov structure of the lineage), but they form a mixing sequence. By the Azuma-Hoeffding inequality for mixing sequences:

\Pr\left[|f_n - f| > \epsilon\right] \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{n\epsilon^2}{2}\right)

This establishes the concentration bound. ∎

