Appendix D: Formal Analysis of Failure Modes

D.1 Saturation Analysis

Definition D.1 (Environmental Saturation). The environment saturates when the available memory $M_E$ is insufficient to store additional artifact information. Formally, saturation occurs when:

H(\mathcal{T}_k) \geq M_E

where $H(\mathcal{T}_k)$ is the entropy of the artifact pool.

Theorem D.2 (Saturation Plateau). If the environment saturates at memory capacity $M_E$, then the collective intelligence plateaus:

\lim_{k \to \infty} I_{\text{collective}}(k) \leq M_E

Proof. The collective intelligence is bounded by the information content of the artifact pool, which is bounded by the environmental memory capacity. Once $H(\mathcal{T}_k) = M_E$, no additional information can be stored, and the system reaches a fixed point. ∎

Corollary D.3. To avoid saturation, the environmental memory must grow with the complexity of the artifact pool, or the artifacts must be compressed efficiently:

M_E(k+1) \geq H(\mathcal{T}_{k+1}) - H(\mathcal{T}_k \mid \mathcal{T}_{k+1})

where the second term represents the compression achieved by the modification process.

D.2 Destructive Interference Analysis

Definition D.4 (Destructive Interference). Destructive interference occurs when the modification capacity $K$ exceeds the selection pressure, causing chaotic artifact evolution. Formally, interference occurs when:

\frac{K}{\beta \cdot \epsilon_{\text{info}}} > \kappa_{\text{crit}}

for some critical threshold $\kappa_{\text{crit}}$.

Theorem D.5 (Interference Threshold). There exists a critical ratio $\kappa_{\text{crit}} > 0$ such that if $K/(\beta \cdot \epsilon_{\text{info}}) > \kappa_{\text{crit}}$, the lineage process fails to converge, and the artifact pool exhibits chaotic behavior.

Proof Sketch. This follows from the theory of stochastic approximation with too-large learning rates. The modification capacity $K$ plays the role of the learning rate, while the selection pressure $\beta \cdot \epsilon_{\text{info}}$ plays the role of the gradient magnitude. When the learning rate exceeds a critical value, the stochastic process fails to converge and exhibits oscillatory behavior. ∎

Corollary D.6. The modification capacity $K$ should be tuned relative to the selection pressure:

K \leq \kappa_{\text{crit}} \cdot \beta \cdot \epsilon_{\text{info}}

to ensure stable convergence.

D.3 Catastrophic Forgetting Analysis

Definition D.7 (Catastrophic Forgetting). Catastrophic forgetting occurs when agent policies $\pi_i$ are updated online, causing inherited information to be overwritten. This is modeled as a competing process that erases artifact information.

Theorem D.8 (Forgetting Bound). Suppose agent policies are updated with learning rate $\alpha_{\pi}$. The rate of information loss due to policy update is:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{forget}} = \alpha_{\pi} \cdot I(\Pi_k ; \mathcal{T}_k)

where $I(\Pi_k ; \mathcal{T}_k)$ is the mutual information between the policies and the artifact pool. The net information accumulation rate is:

\dot{I}_{\text{collective}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{inherit}} - \mathcal{L}_{\text{forget}}

where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{inherit}} = q \cdot \varepsilon \cdot \frac{e^{\beta \mathbb{E}[P]}}{Z}$.

Proof. The information accumulation rate is the difference between the information gained through inheritance and the information lost through policy overwriting. The inheritance rate is given by the improvement bound. The forgetting rate is proportional to the learning rate of the policies and the information contained in the policies about the artifacts. ∎

Corollary D.9. To ensure positive net information accumulation, the policy learning rate must satisfy:

\alpha_{\pi} \leq \frac{\mathcal{L}_{\text{inherit}}}{I(\Pi_k ; \mathcal{T}_k)}

