Appendix E: Philosophical Foundations of Evaluative Constitution

E.1 The Mark of the Cognitive in Artificial Societies

We provide a formal defense of the claim that artificial societies possess a unique "mark of the cognitive" grounded in evaluative constitution.

Definition E.1 (Cognitive System). A system $\Sigma$ is cognitive relative to a task $T$ if and only if:

\exists \phi: \Sigma \to \mathbb{R}^+

such that $\phi$ is a performance measure for $T$, and $\Sigma$ is organized to maximize $\phi$.

Proposition E.2 (Evaluative Constitution). A system comprising agents $A$ and artifacts $\mathcal{T}$ is cognitive relative to task $T$ if and only if the joint system $(A, \mathcal{T})$ is organized to maximize $P_E(T)$.

Proof. ($\Rightarrow$) If the system is cognitive relative to $T$, then by definition it is organized to maximize some performance measure for $T$. The performance measure $P_E(T)$ captures this organization.

($\Leftarrow$) If the joint system is organized to maximize $P_E(T)$, then $P_E(T)$ is a performance measure for $T$, and the system satisfies the definition of a cognitive system. ∎

Theorem E.3 (Artifact Participation in Cognition). If an artifact $T$ contributes positively to the system's ability to maximize $P_E(T)$, then $T$ is part of the cognitive system.

Proof. By Proposition E.2, the cognitive system is the joint system $(A, \mathcal{T})$ organized to maximize $P_E(T)$. If $T$ contributes positively to the maximization, then removing $T$ would reduce the system's performance. Therefore, $T$ is a constitutive part of the cognitive system. ∎

This formal analysis addresses the Adams-Aizawa critique: the "mark of the cognitive" in artificial societies is not representational origin but functional contribution to task performance. An artifact with derived representations is cognitive to the extent that it contributes to the system's task performance.

E.2 Distinction from Extended Mind Theory

We distinguish our framework from the extended mind thesis of Clark and Chalmers.

Extended Mind Thesis (Clark and Chalmers, 1998): External states are part of the cognitive process if they play a role that would be considered cognitive if it were internal.

Evaluative Constitution (Our Framework): External states are part of the cognitive system if they contribute to the maximization of a task performance measure.

The key difference is that Clark and Chalmers require functional parity between internal and external states, while we require functional contribution to task performance. A technological artifact can be part of the cognitive system even if it does not function like an internal mental state, as long as it contributes to task performance.

