Appendix H: Proofs of Corollaries

H.1 Proof of Corollary 1 (Communication-Independent Coordination)

Statement. If technological information can propagate through persistent environmental states, then direct communication is not a necessary condition for collective coordination.

Proof. Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a multi-agent system satisfying the conditions of the Ecological Inheritance Theorem, with $C_{ij} = 0$ for all $i \neq j$. By the theorem, there exists a lineage $T_0 \to T_1 \to \cdots$ with $H_T(k) > 0$ for a positive fraction of generations. This implies that the artifact pool contains information about the collective's problem-solving history, even though no agent communicated directly with any other agent. Therefore, the collective has achieved coordination (manifested as the organized evolution of the artifact pool) without direct communication. ∎

H.2 Proof of Corollary 2 (Technological Persistence)

Statement. If $\tau_T > \tau_A$, then a technological artifact can persist beyond its originating agent.

Proof. By definition, $\tau_T$ is the functional lifetime of the technology, and $\tau_A$ is the functional lifetime of an agent. If $\tau_T > \tau_A$, then there exists a time interval $[t_0, t_0 + \tau_T]$ during which the technology remains functional, and a strict subinterval $[t_0, t_0 + \tau_A]$ during which the originating agent is functional. The remaining interval $[t_0 + \tau_A, t_0 + \tau_T]$ is non-empty and represents the period during which the technology functions without its originating agent. Therefore, the technology persists beyond its originating agent. ∎

H.3 Proof of Corollary 3 (Post-Agent Functional Persistence)

Statement. If $P(\Delta) > 0$ for a non-trivial interval $\Delta$, then the technological ecosystem possesses post-agent functional persistence.

Proof. The condition $P(\Delta) > 0$ states that with positive probability, the artifact pool remains functional $\Delta$ time steps after all agents are removed. By Definition of post-agent functional persistence, this is precisely the condition required. The trivial case $P(0) = 1$ (the pool is functional immediately after agent removal) is excluded by the requirement of a non-trivial interval $\Delta > 0$. ∎

