Appendix I: Comprehensive Reference Analysis

I.1 Verification of Cited Works

We provide a detailed verification of the cited works and their relationship to our framework.

Praxist (Li et al., 2026, arXiv:2608.25955):

· Relevance: Direct empirical validation of cumulative artifact lineages in autonomous R&D.
· Relationship to our work: Praxist provides the engineering; our framework provides the information-theoretic foundations.
· Key overlap: Artifact lineage propagation, generational state transition, post-agent persistence, modification operators.
· Key distinction: Praxist does not provide information-theoretic bounds, capacity analysis, or formal conditions for inheritance.

SwarmWorld (Buehler et al., 2026, arXiv:2608.26081):

· Relevance: Empirical demonstration of technological evolution through environmental inheritance.
· Relationship to our work: SwarmWorld is a minimal validation of the theoretical predictions.
· Key overlap: Post-agent resilience, multi-author lineages, no direct communication.
· Key distinction: SwarmWorld does not formalize the information-theoretic conditions or provide convergence bounds.

TerraLingua (Paolo et al., 2026, arXiv:2603.16910):

· Relevance: Studies cumulative cultural accumulation in LLM ecologies.
· Relationship to our work: TerraLingua focuses on textual transmission; our framework generalizes to any persistent environmental artifact.
· Key overlap: Cumulative cultural evolution in artificial agents.
· Key distinction: TerraLingua does not distinguish between stigmergic coordination and cumulative inheritance, or provide formal conditions for the transition.

MEDAL-ADR (DeepMind, 2023):

· Relevance: Provides a metric for cultural transmission in RL and identifies non-stationarity as a key challenge.
· Relationship to our work: Our framework incorporates non-stationarity as a failure mode and boundary condition.
· Key overlap: Cumulative cultural transmission in artificial agents, curriculum design for stability.
· Key distinction: MEDAL-ADR is an RL framework; our framework is agent-agnostic and applies to LLM agents.

I.2 Gaps in the Literature

Our review of the literature reveals the following gaps that our framework addresses:

1. Information-theoretic conditions for inheritance: No existing work provides formal necessary or sufficient conditions for environmental inheritance in artificial societies.
2. Capacity bounds: No existing work derives information-theoretic limits on the rate of inheritance.
3. Distinction between stigmergy and inheritance: No existing work formally distinguishes between stigmergic coordination and cumulative technological inheritance.
4. Post-agent persistence: No existing work formally analyzes the post-agent persistence regime or derives persistence bounds.
5. Philosophical foundations: No existing work addresses the philosophical status of environmentally distributed cognition in artificial societies.

Our framework fills these gaps and provides a theoretical foundation for interpreting empirical results from Praxist, SwarmWorld, TerraLingua, and MEDAL-ADR.

